OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026", "title": "Generalized Star Height at Most Three", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/article.pdf", "family": "134", "family_title": "Generalized star height at most three", "abstract_html": "Every regular language over a finite alphabet has generalized star height at most three, with complement taken in the same free monoid. We express finite-monoid computations using a prefix code of word pieces.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every regular language \\(L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*\\), \\( h_\\Sigma(L)\\le 3. \\) The expression uses the letters of \\(\\Sigma\\) itself.", "statement": "<p>**Generalized star height at most three — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Sigma\\) be a finite alphabet.</p><p class=\"li\">A generalized regular expression over \\(\\Sigma\\) uses \\(0,1\\), the singleton letters of \\(\\Sigma\\), union, concatenation, complement, and Kleene star. The constants denote \\(\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\{\\varepsilon\\}\\), and complement is always relative to \\(\\Sigma^*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its height is defined by\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ht}(0)=\\operatorname{ht}(1)=\\operatorname{ht}(a)=0,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ht}(P\\cup Q)=\\operatorname{ht}(PQ)\n  =\\max\\{\\operatorname{ht}(P),\\operatorname{ht}(Q)\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ht}(\\neg P)=\\operatorname{ht}(P),\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{ht}(P^*)=1+\\operatorname{ht}(P).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a regular language \\(L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*\\), let \\(h_\\Sigma(L)\\) be the least height of an expression defining \\(L\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every regular language \\(L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*\\),\n\\[\nh_\\Sigma(L)\\le 3.\n\\]\nThe expression uses the letters of \\(\\Sigma\\) itself. Its finite parameters and length may depend on \\(L\\); no bound on expression size or running time is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\Sigma=\\varnothing\\), then \\(\\Sigma^*=\\{\\varepsilon\\}\\), and its only languages are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), both of height zero. Assume \\(\\Sigma\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p>Every regular language is recognized by a morphism\n\\[\nT:\\Sigma^*\\to M\n\\]\ninto a finite monoid: for a deterministic finite automaton, take the monoid of state transformations induced by words, including the identity. Its language is the inverse image of an accepting subset \\(J\\subseteq M\\), hence a finite union of fibers.</p><p>Use the construction of Propositions 6–10 for this morphism. Proposition 11 supplies the height bounds required by the split identity.</p><p>First apply Proposition 1 to the inner split of Proposition 10 on \\(D\\), with empty skip domain. The empty-domain graph languages are \\(1\\) for equal states and \\(0\\) otherwise, so have height zero. The graph languages for composites of \\(G_e\\) on \\(D^*\\) consequently have height at most two.</p><p>Their linear parts are \\(\\widehat F_e\\). Apply Boolean affine recovery from Proposition 2 to obtain graph languages for products of these linear parts at the same height. Restricting inputs and outputs to \\((v,1)\\), \\((y,1)\\) then gives every graph language for composites of \\(F_e\\) on \\(D^*\\), still at height at most two.</p><p>Use these as the skip graph languages for the outer split of Proposition 8. They concern the same deterministic updates \\(F_e\\), including the updates on skipped episodes. A second application of Proposition 1 gives graph languages for their composites on \\(E^*\\) of height at most three:\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow2\\longrightarrow3.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Boolean affine recovery again to remove the translations of \\(F_e\\). Their linear parts are exactly the right actions of the original episode monoid products. The identity basis vector distinguishes monoid elements:\n\\[\ne_{1_M}d=e_d.\n\\]\nThus, for every \\(d\\in M\\), there is a height-at-most-three test\n\\[\nA_d=E^*\\cap T^{-1}(d).\n\\]</p><p>By Proposition 12, for every \\(f\\in M\\),\n\\[\nB_f=N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\cap T^{-1}(f)\n\\]\nhas height at most one, and every word has an episode–remainder factorization.</p><p>The expression\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{\\substack{d,f\\in M\\\\df\\in J}}A_dB_f\n\\]\ndefines \\(T^{-1}(J)\\). Existence of the episode–remainder factorization gives completeness. Every accepted factorization has product \\(df\\in J\\), giving soundness.</p><p>Finite union and concatenation preserve the maximum height, which is three. All expressions are over the given alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\), with complement relative to \\(\\Sigma^*\\). Therefore\n\\[\nh_\\Sigma(L)\\le3.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The graph language from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\) on \\(D^*\\) is …", "statement": "<p>**Split identity — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\subseteq\\Sigma^+\\) be a prefix code: no member is a proper prefix of another.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D'\\subseteq D\\subseteq E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Give each \\(e\\in D\\) a deterministic total update \\(G_e:S\\to S\\) of a fixed finite state set \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(w=e_1\\cdots e_r\\in D^*\\), let \\(G_w\\) apply the updates in reading order; \\(G_\\varepsilon\\) is the identity.</p><p class=\"li\">A graph language from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\) on a specified episode domain consists of the words in that domain whose composite update sends \\(x\\) to \\(y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(P_x,Q_y\\subseteq\\Sigma^*\\), for \\(x,y\\in S\\), satisfy:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. If \\(w\\in D^*\\) has a prefix \\(uv\\) with \\(u\\in P_x\\) and \\(v\\in Q_y\\), then \\(w\\) has a first episode \\(e\\), \\(uv=e\\), and \\(G_e(x)=y\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. For every \\(e\\in D\\setminus D'\\) and every \\(x\\in S\\),\n     \\[\n     e\\in P_xQ_{G_e(x)}.\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_{x,y}\\) be the graph languages for these same updates on \\((D')^*\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The graph language from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\) on \\(D^*\\) is\n\\[\nD^*\\cap\n\\left[\nW_{x,y}\\ \\cup\\\n\\left(\\bigcup_{i\\in S}W_{x,i}P_i\\right)\n\\left(\\bigcup_{j,i\\in S}Q_jW_{j,i}P_i\\right)^*\n\\left(\\bigcup_{l\\in S}Q_lW_{l,y}\\right)\n\\right].\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\), all \\(P_x\\), and all \\(Q_y\\) have height at most one, and all \\(W_{x,y}\\) have height at most \\(H\\ge0\\), these graph languages have height at most\n\\[\n\\max\\{2,1+H\\}.\n\\]\nIn particular, when \\(D'=\\varnothing\\), the bound is two.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every word in \\(E^*\\) has a unique factorization into episodes. Indeed, the first factors of two proposed factorizations are prefix comparable, hence equal by the prefix-code property; induction handles the remaining factors. The empty word has the empty factorization.</p><p>Fix a word in the right-hand side of (2). Membership in \\(D^*\\) fixes its ambient episode factorization, but does not by itself align an arbitrary interior factor with that factorization. Establish alignment in order, beginning at the start of the word.</p><p>The first \\(W\\)-factor starts at a true boundary. Since its words belong to \\((D')^*\\), its own first episode and the ambient first episode are prefix comparable and both belong to \\(E\\), so they are equal. Repeating this argument shows that the whole \\(W\\)-factor consumes complete ambient episodes. Its endpoint is another true boundary, and its graph indices record their actual composite update.</p><p>For a factorization through the second term of (2), the first \\(P\\)-factor and the following \\(Q\\)-factor therefore begin at a true boundary. Assumption 1 says that their concatenation is exactly the next episode and has the indicated update. The next \\(W\\)-factor begins at its true endpoint, so the prefix-code argument applies again. Alternating these steps establishes every boundary and propagates every state. A zero-fold middle star leaves one \\(P,Q\\) pair, to which the same argument applies. The first term \\(W_{x,y}\\) already records the required composite on its domain.</p><p>Conversely, follow the actual computation on a word of \\(D^*\\). Split every episode outside \\(D'\\) using Assumption 2, and group intervening runs of episodes from \\(D'\\) into \\(W\\)-factors with their actual endpoint states. This gives the second term of (2). If no episode lies outside \\(D'\\), the word belongs to \\(W_{x,y}\\). This includes \\(\\varepsilon\\). Empty domains are allowed; if \\(S\\) is empty, there are no indexed assertions.</p><p>For the height estimate, \\(D^*\\) has height at most two. Every factor inside the middle star has height at most \\(\\max\\{1,H\\}\\), so that star has height at most \\(1+\\max\\{1,H\\}\\). Finite unions, concatenations, and intersections preserve the maximum height. The bound is consequently \\(\\max\\{2,1+H\\}\\).</p><p>When \\(D'=\\varnothing\\), \\(W_{x,y}\\) is \\(1\\) if \\(x=y\\), and \\(0\\) otherwise, so its height is zero. Assumption 1 also rules out a successful pair on the empty word. It applies even when the two factors make independent, disagreeing auxiliary choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Graph languages for products of the linear parts \\(L_e\\) are obtained from the \\(R_{x,z}\\) by finite Boolean operations, without increasing a common height bound.", "statement": "<p>**Affine recovery and homogeneous lifting.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\subseteq\\Sigma^+\\) be a prefix code and \\(D\\subseteq E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a finite vector space over a finite field \\(\\mathbb F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(e\\in D\\), let\n  \\[\n  G_e(v)=vL_e+b_e,\n  \\qquad L_e\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{lin}}(S),\\quad b_e\\in S,\n  \\]\n  with both terms determined by \\(e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Vectors are written on the left of their maps; products apply maps in reading order.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R_{x,z}\\) be the graph languages of the affine composites on \\(D^*\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Graph languages for products of the linear parts \\(L_e\\) are obtained from the \\(R_{x,z}\\) by finite Boolean operations, without increasing a common height bound.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Each affine update has a linear homogeneous lift on \\(S\\oplus\\mathbb F\\). Products of the lifts recover the affine composites on the slice with last coordinate \\(1\\), again without increasing height.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 removes the translation of a whole affine composite by testing its outputs on \\(x\\) and on zero on the same word. Lemma 2.2 realizes each affine composite as a slice of a product of linear maps on the enlarged space. Both operations preserve any common generalized star-height bound, giving the two assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Boolean affine recovery — original Lemma 2.2.** Under the assumptions of Proposition 2, the graph language for the product of the linear parts, from \\(x\\in S\\) to \\(y\\in S\\), is …", "statement": "<p>**Boolean affine recovery — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 2, the graph language for the product of the linear parts, from \\(x\\in S\\) to \\(y\\in S\\), is\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{b\\in S}\\left(R_{x,y+b}\\cap R_{0,b}\\right).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nA common height bound for the \\(R_{x,z}\\) is preserved.</p>", "proof": "<p>Composition in reading order obeys\n\\[\n(vL+b)A+c=vLA+(bA+c).\n\\]\nInduction from the identity on the empty word writes each word’s composite as \\(v\\mapsto vL+b\\), where \\(L\\) is exactly the product of its episode linear parts.</p><p>For that word, the output at zero is \\(b\\), and the output at \\(x\\) is \\(y+b\\) exactly when \\(xL=y\\). Requiring both facts on the same word gives the intersection in (3); the finite union permits every translation \\(b\\). Finite Boolean operations preserve the asserted height bound. No invertibility is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Homogeneous lift — original Lemma 2.3.** Let \\(S\\) be a finite vector space over a finite field \\(\\mathbb F\\), and let \\( F(v)=vL+b. \\) Its homogeneous lift on \\(S\\oplus\\mathbb F\\) is \\( \\widehat F(v,c)=(vL+cb,c). \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Homogeneous lift — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be a finite vector space over a finite field \\(\\mathbb F\\), and let\n\\[\nF(v)=vL+b.\n\\]\nIts homogeneous lift on \\(S\\oplus\\mathbb F\\) is\n\\[\n\\widehat F(v,c)=(vL+cb,c).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThis map is linear. Products of such lifts are the lifts of the corresponding affine composites in reading order. Graph languages for products of lifts give those for affine composites by using inputs \\((v,1)\\) and outputs \\((y,1)\\), without increasing height.</p>", "proof": "<p>The displayed map is linear in \\((v,c)\\). If \\(F(v)=vL+b\\) is followed by \\(G(v)=vA+d\\), their lifts give\n\\[\n(v,c)\\longmapsto(vLA+c(bA+d),c),\n\\]\nthe lift of the affine composite. Induction proves the product assertion; the slice \\(c=1\\) proves the graph assertion. The empty product is the identity in both spaces. No invertibility is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists an integer \\(l\\ge1\\), depending only on \\(|A|\\) and \\(|S|\\), such that for every function \\( f:A^l\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{lin}}(S) \\) there is a function \\(\\beta_f:A^l\\to S\\) with the …", "statement": "<p>**Shift table — original Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a nonempty finite set.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a finite vector space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists an integer \\(l\\ge1\\), depending only on \\(|A|\\) and \\(|S|\\), such that for every function\n\\[\nf:A^l\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{lin}}(S)\n\\]\nthere is a function \\(\\beta_f:A^l\\to S\\) with the following property.</p><p>For every tuple \\(a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_l)\\in A^l\\) and every \\(x\\in S\\), some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,l\\}\\) makes\n\\[\nb\\longmapsto\nxf(a_1,\\ldots,a_{i-1},b,a_{i+1},\\ldots,a_l)\n+\\beta_f(a_1,\\ldots,a_{i-1},b,a_{i+1},\\ldots,a_l)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nconstant on \\(A\\).</p><p>The integer \\(l\\) is uniform over \\(f\\), and \\(\\beta_f\\) is independent of \\(x\\). The successful coordinate may depend on both the tuple and \\(x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(a=|A|\\) and \\(q=|S|\\). If \\(a=1\\) or \\(q=1\\), take \\(l=1\\) and \\(\\beta_f=0\\). Assume \\(a,q&gt;1\\).</p><p>For an integer \\(l\\) to be chosen, select independent uniform vectors \\(\\beta(c)\\in S\\) at all cells \\(c\\in A^l\\). Fix an arbitrary table \\(f\\). For \\(x\\in S\\) and \\(a\\in A^l\\), let \\(B_{x,a}\\) be the event that none of the \\(l\\) coordinate lines through \\(a\\) has the required constancy.</p><p>Condition on \\(\\beta(a)\\). For a specified line, constancy forces exactly one value of \\(\\beta\\) at each of its other \\(a-1\\) cells, regardless of \\(f\\). Its success probability is \\(q^{1-a}\\). Different lines share only their central cell, so their successes are conditionally independent. The conditional failure probability is independent of the central value. Thus\n\\[\n\\Pr(B_{x,a})=(1-q^{1-a})^l=:p_l.\n\\]</p><p>The event depends only on values at Hamming distance at most one from \\(a\\). Events whose centers have distance greater than two use disjoint random variables, giving independence from their joint family. A dependency neighborhood has size at most\n\\[\nq\\left(1+l(a-1)+\\binom l2(a-1)^2\\right)-1\n\\le D_l:=q(1+la+l^2a^2).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe factor \\(q\\) includes every input vector at each center.</p><p>Apply Lemma 3.1 with\n\\[\nD=D_l,\\qquad s=\\frac1{2(D_l+1)}.\n\\]\nBernoulli’s inequality gives\n\\[\n(1-s)^{D_l}\\ge1-D_ls\\ge\\frac12,\n\\qquad\ns(1-s)^{D_l}\\ge\\frac1{4(D_l+1)}.\n\\]\nThe probability \\(p_l\\) decays exponentially in \\(l\\), whereas \\(D_l\\) grows quadratically. Choose \\(l\\) so that\n\\[\np_l\\le\\frac1{4(D_l+1)}.\n\\]\nThis choice depends only on \\(a,q\\) and works for every \\(f\\). Avoiding every \\(B_{x,a}\\) gives the required correction simultaneously for all tuples and inputs.</p><p>Fix one successful \\(\\beta_f\\) for each of the finitely many possible tables at these dimensions. The correction is now determined by the table and tuple before any splitting choices. The argument neither requires one coordinate to work for all inputs nor restricts \\(f\\) to tables arising from feasible word prefixes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite dependency argument used in the proof of original Lemma 2.4.** Let a finite family of events have a dependency graph such that each event is independent of the joint family of its nonneighbors and has at most …", "statement": "<p>**Finite dependency argument used in the proof of original Lemma 2.4.**</p><p>Let a finite family of events have a dependency graph such that each event is independent of the joint family of its nonneighbors and has at most \\(D\\) neighbors. Suppose every event has probability at most\n\\[\ns(1-s)^D\n\\]\nfor some \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\).</p><p>Then the probability that none of the events occurs is positive.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on the size of a set of other events conditioned not to occur. Along with positivity of the conditioning events, prove that the conditional probability of any remaining event is at most \\(s\\).</p><p>For an empty conditioning set this follows from the assumed bound. Positivity for a set of size \\(k\\) follows by deleting one event and applying the conditional bound for a set of size \\(k-1\\).</p><p>To prove the conditional bound at size \\(k\\), split the conditioning set into neighbors \\(J_1\\) and nonneighbors \\(J_0\\) of the event \\(B\\). Write \\(C_i\\) for simultaneous nonoccurrence of \\(J_i\\). If \\(J_1\\) is empty, joint independence gives the unconditional probability. Otherwise,\n\\[\n\\Pr(B\\mid C_0\\cap C_1)\n\\le\n\\frac{\\Pr(B\\mid C_0)}{\\Pr(C_1\\mid C_0)}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\Pr(B)}{(1-s)^{|J_1|}}\n\\le s.\n\\]\nFor the denominator, expose the events in \\(J_1\\) one at a time. Each resulting conditioning set has size at most \\(k-1\\), so each nonoccurrence probability is at least \\(1-s\\). The numerator uses joint independence from \\(J_0\\).</p><p>This completes the induction. Successive conditioning gives positive probability of full avoidance.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a finite-dimensional torus \\(T\\), sets \\(I_\\alpha,J_\\alpha\\subseteq T\\) for \\(\\alpha\\in\\Lambda\\), a set \\(W\\subseteq T\\), roster points \\(v_p\\in T\\) for \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\), and constants \\(\\rho,\\eta>0\\) such …", "statement": "<p>**Robust tag clock — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Lambda\\) and \\(\\mathcal P\\) be finite nonempty sets, and put \\(m=|\\Lambda|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\|x\\|_{\\mathbb T}=\\min_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}|x-j|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A finite product \\(T=\\mathbb T^C\\) has maximum metric\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{dist}(u,v)=\\max_{c\\in C}\\|u_c-v_c\\|_{\\mathbb T}.\n  \\]\n  Addition and subtraction are coordinatewise. For sets, \\(I+J\\) is their sumset and \\(z-J=\\{z-j:j\\in J\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All logarithms below are natural.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a finite-dimensional torus \\(T\\), sets \\(I_\\alpha,J_\\alpha\\subseteq T\\) for \\(\\alpha\\in\\Lambda\\), a set \\(W\\subseteq T\\), roster points \\(v_p\\in T\\) for \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\), and constants \\(\\rho,\\eta&gt;0\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every \\(z\\in T\\),\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal E_z=\\{(\\alpha,\\lambda)\\in\\Lambda^2:z\\in I_\\alpha+J_\\lambda\\}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   is either contained in the diagonal or consists of one off-diagonal pair.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(z\\notin W\\), then for every \\(\\alpha\\in\\Lambda\\),\n   \\[\n   I_\\alpha\\cap(z-J_\\alpha)\n   \\]\n   contains a ball of radius \\(\\rho\\). In particular, \\(\\mathcal E_z\\) is the full diagonal.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every family \\((\\widetilde v_p)_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(\\widetilde v_p,v_p)&lt;\\eta\n   \\quad(p\\in\\mathcal P),\n   \\]\n   and every \\(a\\in T\\),\n   \\[\n   \\#\\{(p,p')\\in\\mathcal P^2:p\\ne p',\n   \\ a+\\widetilde v_{p'}-\\widetilde v_p\\in W\\}\n   \\le c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\log(2m),\n   \\qquad c_{\\mathrm{cl}}=10^7.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]</p><p>The dimension, \\(\\rho\\), and \\(\\eta\\) may depend on both finite sets; \\(c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\) is absolute.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\na_0=\\lceil64\\log(2m)\\rceil,\\qquad\nb_0=\\lceil\\log_2m\\rceil,\\qquad F=400.\n\\]</p><p>**Partitions and coordinates.**\nChoose \\(a_0\\) left/right partitions of \\(\\Lambda\\) such that whenever\n\\[\n\\alpha\\ne\\lambda,\\qquad\n\\{\\alpha,\\lambda\\}\\ne\\{\\gamma,\\nu\\},\n\\]\nsome partition puts \\(\\alpha\\) on the left, \\(\\lambda\\) on the right, and \\(\\gamma,\\nu\\) on the same side. This includes \\(\\gamma=\\nu\\).</p><p>For a random partition, each constraint is feasible even with coincident displayed tags: the excluded set equality prevents putting \\(\\gamma,\\nu\\) together from forcing \\(\\alpha,\\lambda\\) together. At most four tag choices are involved, so its success probability is at least \\(1/16\\). There are at most \\(m^4\\) constraints. The expected number missed by \\(a_0\\) independent partitions is at most\n\\[\nm^4(15/16)^{a_0}\n\\le m^4e^{-a_0/16}\n\\le m^4(2m)^{-4}\n=\\frac1{16}&lt;1.\n\\]\nA choice missing none exists. Give each such partition one first circle coordinate.</p><p>Also choose \\(b_0\\) partitions into Types 1 and 2 separating every distinct pair of tags, by assigning distinct binary strings. Give each Type partition a batch of coordinates. For every ordered list of \\(F\\) directed pairs of distinct roster slots whose underlying unordered edges are distinct and form a forest, include a dedicated coordinate in the batch. Add spare coordinates to make its size positive and odd. There are finitely many such lists; a one-coordinate spare batch is allowed when there are none. These coordinates form \\(C\\). When \\(m=1\\), there are no Type batches, but there are first coordinates.</p><p>**First coordinates.**\nEnumerate \\(\\mathcal P=\\{p_0,\\ldots,p_{r-1}\\}\\). In each first coordinate put\n\\[\nv_{p_j}=\\theta2^j\\pmod1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta&gt;0\\) makes all values lie in an arc of length less than \\(1/8\\). Ordered differences between distinct slots are nonzero and pairwise distinct. Indeed, a positive difference\n\\[\n2^j-2^i=2^i(2^{j-i}-1)\n\\]\ndetermines \\(i\\) from its power of two and then determines \\(j\\); the sign distinguishes the reverse orientation. The small scale prevents collisions modulo one.</p><p>Choose \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1/100\\) so that these differences are separated from each other and from zero by more than \\(12\\epsilon\\), with a strict margin. If there are no distinct-slot pairs, any sufficiently small positive \\(\\epsilon\\) works.</p><p>In the coordinate for a left/right partition, impose\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n\\text{side of }\\alpha&amp;I_\\alpha&amp;J_\\alpha\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{left}&amp;\\|i\\|_{\\mathbb T}\\le\\epsilon&amp;\\|j\\|_{\\mathbb T}\\ge4\\epsilon\\\\\n\\text{right}&amp;\\|i\\|_{\\mathbb T}\\ge4\\epsilon&amp;\\|j\\|_{\\mathbb T}\\le\\epsilon.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nA left-to-right edge forces its total to have norm at most \\(2\\epsilon\\). A same-side edge has one near and one far summand, so its total has norm at least \\(3\\epsilon\\). These possibilities are disjoint.</p><p>If \\(\\|z\\|_{\\mathbb T}&gt;6\\epsilon\\), every tag has a diagonal decomposition by assigning zero to the near summand and \\(z\\) to the far summand. Moving the first summand by at most \\(\\epsilon/2\\) and the second oppositely preserves both requirements.</p><p>**Type 1 batches.**\nPut\n\\[\nC_I=\\{0,1,3\\}/6,\\qquad C_J=\\{2,4,5\\}/6.\n\\]\nA Type 1 tag requires each coordinate of its \\(I\\)-vector to be within \\(1/16\\) of \\(C_I\\), and each coordinate of its \\(J\\)-vector within \\(1/16\\) of \\(C_J\\).</p><p>The center sums contain all six multiples of \\(1/6\\). For any coordinate total \\(z\\), choose a center sum \\(c_I+c_J\\) with signed error \\(|\\delta|\\le1/12\\). Then\n\\[\nz=(c_I+\\delta/2)+(c_J+\\delta/2)\n\\]\nputs each summand within \\(1/24\\) of its center and leaves margin \\(1/48\\). Moving the first summand by at most \\(1/96\\) and the second oppositely preserves validity.</p><p>The center sets have circular distance at least \\(1/6\\), so differences of allowed Type 1 vectors satisfy\n\\[\n\\|i_c-j_c\\|_{\\mathbb T}\n\\ge\\frac16-\\frac2{16}\n=\\frac1{24}\n\\quad\\text{in every batch coordinate.}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]</p><p>**Type 2 batches.**\nIn a batch of \\(2s+1\\) coordinates, a Type 2 tag requires each of its \\(I\\)- and \\(J\\)-vectors separately to have at least \\(s+1\\) coordinates of norm at most \\(1/100\\).</p><p>If a total \\(z\\) has one coordinate \\(c_0\\) of norm at most \\(1/200\\), put the first summand equal to \\(z_{c_0}\\) and the second equal to zero there. Partition the other \\(2s\\) coordinates into groups of size \\(s\\); put the first summand equal to zero in one group and the second equal to zero in the other. The remaining summands are determined by \\(z\\). Each vector has \\(s+1\\) designated small coordinates, with margin at least \\(1/200\\). Opposite perturbations of norm at most \\(1/400\\) preserve both majority conditions.</p><p>Conversely, the small-coordinate sets of two allowed Type 2 vectors intersect, so their difference has a coordinate satisfying\n\\[\n\\|i_c-j_c\\|_{\\mathbb T}\\le\\frac2{100}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nThis includes a one-coordinate batch.</p><p>**Compatibility and interior.**\nDefine \\(I_\\alpha,J_\\alpha\\) by all these coordinate requirements. If an off-diagonal edge \\(\\alpha\\to\\lambda\\) occurs, a suitable first-coordinate partition excludes every competitor \\((\\gamma,\\nu)\\) except possibly its reversal. If both orientations occurred, their witnesses would give\n\\[\ni_\\alpha+j_\\lambda=i_\\lambda+j_\\alpha,\n\\qquad\ni_\\alpha-j_\\alpha=i_\\lambda-j_\\lambda.\n\\]\nA Type partition separating \\(\\alpha,\\lambda\\) would make this common difference satisfy both (9) and (10), contradicting \\(2/100&lt;1/24\\). This proves Assertion 1.</p><p>Let \\(W\\) be the union of the obstructions:</p><p class=\"li\">some first coordinate has norm at most \\(6\\epsilon\\);</p><p class=\"li\">some Type batch has no coordinate of norm at most \\(1/200\\).</p><p>Outside \\(W\\), all diagonal constructions apply and combine across the disjoint blocks. For example,\n\\[\n\\rho=\\min\\{\\epsilon/2,1/96,1/400\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\ngives a ball in \\(I_\\alpha\\cap(z-J_\\alpha)\\) for every tag. Assertion 1 then makes the graph the full diagonal, proving Assertion 2.</p><p>**Roster values in dedicated coordinates.**\nFor the coordinate dedicated to a forest list\n\\[\n((p_j,p'_j))_{j=0}^{F-1},\n\\]\nprescribe\n\\[\nv_{p'_j}-v_{p_j}=j/F\\pmod1.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nChoose a root in each tree and assign values by traversing its edges. There is no cycle, hence no conflict. Give arbitrary values to other vertices and spare coordinates.</p><p>For every coordinate translate \\(a_c\\), one of the points \\(j/F\\) lies within \\(1/(2F)=1/800\\) of \\(-a_c\\). Choose \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) so small that\n\\[\n1/800+2\\eta&lt;1/200,\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nand so that the ordered distinct-slot differences in each first coordinate remain separated from one another and zero by more than \\(12\\epsilon\\). This is possible by the strict finite margins. Under the permitted perturbations, each dedicated forest list therefore contains a pair whose translated difference is within \\(1/200\\) of zero in its dedicated coordinate.</p><p>**Counting obstructions.**\nFix permitted perturbations and a translate \\(a\\). In a first coordinate, at most one ordered distinct-slot pair can have translated difference within \\(6\\epsilon\\) of zero: two such differences would be at distance at most \\(12\\epsilon\\). First-coordinate obstructions cost at most \\(a_0\\) pairs.</p><p>Fix a Type batch. Consider directed pairs whose translated differences have no coordinate within \\(1/200\\) of zero in that batch. Their underlying simple graph cannot contain a forest with \\(F\\) edges, because its dedicated coordinate would make one chosen orientation close to zero.</p><p>A spanning forest on its nonisolated vertices therefore has \\(f&lt;F\\) edges. If it has \\(v\\) vertices and \\(t\\) components, then\n\\[\nf=v-t,\\qquad t\\le v/2,\n\\]\nso \\(v\\le2f&lt;2F\\). There are fewer than \\(4F^2\\) directed pairs on these vertices. The same bound covers a roster too small to admit a dedicated forest list.</p><p>Thus the number of obstructed pairs is at most \\(a_0+4F^2b_0\\). Since \\(\\log2&gt;1/2\\),\n\\[\na_0\\le66\\log(2m),\\qquad b_0\\le2\\log(2m),\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\na_0+4F^2b_0\n\\le(66+8\\cdot400^2)\\log(2m)\n&lt;10^7\\log(2m).\n\\]\nThis proves Assertion 3, uniformly in every translate and every permitted perturbation. The one-slot roster and \\(m=1\\) cases are covered by the same construction, with the corresponding counts vacuous.</p><p>For later use, a periodic additive map \\(\\varphi:\\mathbb Z\\to T\\) permits a prefix ending at \\(k\\) to test \\(\\varphi(k-t)\\in I_\\alpha\\) and an independent suffix to test \\(\\varphi(q-k)\\in J_\\lambda\\). Once the positions \\(t,q,k\\) agree, the pair belongs to \\(\\mathcal E_{\\varphi(q-t)}\\). Periodicity makes these finite residue-table tests; the torus is not an added input alphabet.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a translation-commuting marker rule of finite radius \\(r\\ge K\\) whose distinct markers have distance at least \\(d\\), and whose absence near a position forces a length-\\(K\\) block there to have a period less …", "statement": "<p>**Local markers and periodic continuation.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Sigma\\) be a nonempty finite alphabet.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(d\\ge2\\) and \\(K&gt;3d^2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A positive integer \\(p\\) is a period of a finite block if letters at positions differing by \\(p\\) agree whenever both lie in the block.</p><p class=\"li\">Letter intervals are half-open; intervals of possible marker positions are inclusive.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a translation-commuting marker rule of finite radius \\(r\\ge K\\) whose distinct markers have distance at least \\(d\\), and whose absence near a position forces a length-\\(K\\) block there to have a period less than \\(d\\).</p><p>The short-period blocks so obtained have unique two-sided short-period continuations. Sufficiently overlapping such blocks have a common continuation; in particular, a marker-free interval yields the single periodic run specified in Lemma 5.2.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 constructs the finite-radius separated marker rule and proves the marker-versus-short-period alternative. Lemma 5.2 establishes uniqueness, common continuation under overlap, and the stated single periodic run on a marker-free interval. Its finite-word argument and rejection convention allow these facts to be applied to the actual letters read by a prefix or suffix.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local markers — original Lemma 4.1.** Under the assumptions of Proposition 5, there exist \\(r\\ge K\\) and a fixed rule assigning markers to every word in \\(\\Sigma^{\\mathbb Z}\\) such that:", "statement": "<p>**Local markers — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 5, there exist \\(r\\ge K\\) and a fixed rule assigning markers to every word in \\(\\Sigma^{\\mathbb Z}\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. distinct markers have distance at least \\(d\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. the decision at \\(x\\) uses only \\([x-r,x+r)\\), and the rule commutes with translations;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. if \\([z-d,z+d]\\) contains no marker, then \\([z,z+K)\\) has a positive period less than \\(d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Order the length-\\(K\\) words with no period below \\(d\\) as \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_N\\). At stage \\(j\\), place a marker at every start of \\(v_j\\) at distance at least \\(d\\) from every earlier marker, retaining earlier markers.</p><p>Two newly placed starts at distance \\(a\\), \\(0&lt;a&lt;d\\), would give overlapping copies of \\(v_j\\). Equality on the overlap says exactly that \\(a\\) is a period of \\(v_j\\), contrary to its inclusion in the list. Both old–new and new–new pairs therefore satisfy the required separation.</p><p>Take\n\\[\nr_0=K,\\qquad r_j=K+j(d-1).\n\\]\nTo decide whether stage \\(j\\) places a marker at \\(x\\), inspect its length-\\(K\\) block and earlier marker status at positions from \\(x-(d-1)\\) to \\(x+(d-1)\\). Inductively, all necessary windows lie in \\([x-r_j,x+r_j)\\). The final radius \\(r=r_N\\) works. If the list is empty, take \\(r=K\\) and no markers. All instructions use relative positions, so the rule commutes with translations.</p><p>If the block at \\(z\\) appeared in the list, its start either received a marker or was prevented by a marker at distance less than \\(d\\). That marker remains. Absence throughout \\([z-d,z+d]\\) therefore means the block was not listed, so it has a positive period below \\(d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Common periodic continuation — original Lemma 4.2.** Two two-sided words with respective positive periods \\(a,b\\) that agree on \\(ab\\) consecutive positions agree everywhere.", "statement": "<p>**Common periodic continuation — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><p>Two two-sided words with respective positive periods \\(a,b\\) that agree on \\(ab\\) consecutive positions agree everywhere.</p><p>Consequently, for \\(d\\ge2\\), \\(K&gt;3d^2\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A length-\\(K\\) block with a period below \\(d\\) determines a unique two-sided periodic continuation having some period below \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A finite nonempty family of such blocks in one word, with start-position diameter \\(w\\) and \\(K-w\\ge d^2\\), determines one common continuation agreeing with the word on their union.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For the rule in Lemma 5.1, if the inclusive interval \\([z,y]\\) has no marker and \\(y-z\\ge2d\\), the word agrees with one two-sided word of period less than \\(d\\) throughout\n   \\[\n   [z+d,y-d+K).\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]</p><p>These statements apply to finite words when all stated blocks and marker inspection windows are present.</p>", "proof": "<p>The integer \\(ab\\) is a period of both two-sided words. Move any position into an interval of \\(ab\\) consecutive positions by adding a multiple of \\(ab\\), without changing either letter. Agreement there proves agreement everywhere.</p><p>A finite block of period \\(a&lt;d\\) extends by repeating its first \\(a\\) letters in both directions. Any two choices \\(a,b&lt;d\\) give continuations agreeing on the block, whose length exceeds \\(ab\\); hence the continuations coincide. This proves Assertion 1.</p><p>For Assertion 2, every pair of blocks overlaps in at least\n\\[\nK-w\\ge d^2&gt;ab\n\\]\npositions, where \\(a,b&lt;d\\) are their short periods. Their continuations agree. Fixing one block identifies a common continuation for the family. Since \\(w&lt;K\\), their union is an interval, all of whose letters agree with it.</p><p>For Assertion 3, each integer \\(x\\) from \\(z+d\\) through \\(y-d\\) has\n\\[\n[x-d,x+d]\\subseteq[z,y].\n\\]\nLemma 5.1 gives a short period for its length-\\(K\\) block. Successive blocks overlap in \\(K-1&gt;d^2\\) letters, so their continuations agree successively. Their union is exactly (14). The common continuation has the short period of the first block. This includes \\(y-z=2d\\), when there is one block.</p><p>For a finite word, extend it arbitrarily to a two-sided word; this is possible because \\(\\Sigma\\ne\\varnothing\\). Internal inspection windows give the same decisions under every extension, and all block equalities use only given letters.</p><p>Accordingly, in every finite test below, a marker inspection is performed only when its entire window is present. Otherwise the whole test rejects. An unavailable window never removes an origin from a comparison or uniqueness test.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "All the choices above are simultaneously possible and depend only on \\(M,\\Sigma\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite schedules for the two splits — construction of original Section 5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a nonempty finite alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\), a finite monoid \\(M\\), and a morphism\n  \\[\n  T:\\Sigma^*\\to M.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For available ordered intervals, write \\(T_{a,b}\\) for their monoid value, with\n  \\[\n  T_{a,a}=1_M,\\qquad T_{a,c}=T_{a,b}T_{b,c}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  V=\\mathbb F_2^M,\\qquad U=V\\oplus\\mathbb F_2,\\qquad N_M=|M|!.\n  \\]\n  With basis \\((e_m)_{m\\in M}\\), let \\(d\\in M\\) act linearly on the right by \\(e_md=e_{md}\\). This action is faithful because \\(e_{1_M}d=e_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose tuple lengths \\(g,g'\\ge1\\) from Proposition 3 for entry set \\(M\\) and state spaces \\(V,U\\), respectively, and fix successful correction tables for all maps at those dimensions.</p><p>Define the finite metadata and schedules as follows.</p><p>**Early kinds and residues.**\nAn early kind is\n\\[\n(j,x,y,d_{-j},\\psi),\n\\quad\n1\\le j\\le g',\\quad x,y\\in U,\\quad\nd_{-j}\\in M^{g'-1},\\quad\n\\psi:M\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{lin}}(U).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nThe list \\(d_{-j}\\) retains the indices and order of all entries except \\(j\\). Every total \\(\\psi\\) is included, without feasibility restrictions.</p><p>Let \\(\\kappa\\) be the number of kinds, make \\(\\mu\\) copies of each, and let the resulting roster have \\(N=\\kappa\\mu\\) slots, ordered in blocks by \\(j\\). Put\n\\[\nB=20(N+1)^3,\\qquad\nr_i=4N^2i+i^2\\pmod B\\quad(1\\le i\\le N).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]</p><p>**Outer tags.**\nA main tag is\n\\[\n(j,u,A,C,d_{-j},X,Y),\n\\quad\n1\\le j\\le g,\\quad 0\\le u&lt;B,\\quad\nA,C\\in M,\\quad d_{-j}\\in M^{g-1},\\quad X,Y\\in V.\n\\]\nA periodic tag is\n\\[\n(j,u,m_p,X,Y),\n\\quad\n0\\le j\\le g,\\quad0\\le u&lt;B,\\quad m_p\\in M,\\quad X,Y\\in V.\n\\]\nThe form is part of the tag. Let \\(\\Lambda\\) be the tag set, \\(m=|\\Lambda|\\), and denote its state fields by \\(X(\\alpha),Y(\\alpha)\\). Thus\n\\[\nm=B|V|^2\\bigl(g|M|^{g+1}+(g+1)|M|\\bigr).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\mu\\) so that\n\\[\n\\mu&gt;20\\bigl(c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\log(2m)+4(g+1)\\bigr)+10,\n\\qquad c_{\\mathrm{cl}}=10^7.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]</p><p>**Clock and early positions.**\nApply Proposition 4 to \\(\\Lambda\\) and the roster, obtaining \\(T,I_\\alpha,J_\\alpha,W,v_p,\\rho,\\eta\\). Choose distinct sufficiently large primes \\(n_i^0&gt;B\\), let \\(\\varphi^0:\\mathbb Z\\to T\\) have coordinate speeds \\(1/n_i^0\\), and set\n\\[\nR=\\prod_i n_i^0,\\qquad w_0=BR.\n\\]\nRealize the roster by increasing positive integer offsets \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_N\\) satisfying\n\\[\np_i\\equiv r_i\\pmod B,\\qquad\n\\varphi^0(p_i)\\ \\text{is within the chosen tolerance of }v_{p_i},\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nwith all ordered nonzero differences separated from one another and from zero by more than \\(2w_0+2\\). Write \\(\\mathcal P\\) for this set with its kind and copy labels and \\(h=\\operatorname{diam}\\mathcal P\\).</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n0=\\beta_0&lt;\\beta_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;\\beta_{g'}\n\\]\nso that each slot of block \\(j\\) lies strictly between \\(\\beta_{j-1}\\) and \\(\\beta_j\\). For beginning \\(s\\), put\n\\[\nb'_j=s+\\beta_j.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]</p><p>**Final clock and inner markers.**\nChoose\n\\[\nd&gt;10(2h+2w_0+5),\\qquad K&gt;3d^2,\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\nand the inner marker rule of Proposition 5 with radius \\(r\\ge K\\).</p><p>Approximate each speed \\(1/n_i^0\\) by \\(a_i/n_i\\), where the \\(n_i\\) are distinct primes greater than \\(d,N_M,B\\), \\(1\\le a_i&lt;n_i\\). Let \\(\\varphi\\) be the resulting additive map and\n\\[\nn=\\prod_i n_i.\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nRequire\n\\[\n\\#\\{(p,p')\\in\\mathcal P^2:p\\ne p',\n\\ a+\\varphi(p')-\\varphi(p)\\in W\\}\n\\le c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\log(2m)\n\\quad(a\\in T),\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nand require\n\\[\n\\{\\varphi(aB):0\\le a&lt;R\\}\n\\quad\\text{to have covering radius less than }\\rho.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]</p><p>Choose positive integers \\(L_{\\mathrm{lag}},H_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\) satisfying\n\\[\nB\\mid L_{\\mathrm{lag}},\\qquad\nL_{\\mathrm{lag}}&gt;r+(|M|+2)d,\\qquad\nH_{\\mathrm{ext}}&gt;L_{\\mathrm{lag}}+ndN_MB+2d.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\nChoose widths \\(H_u\\), \\(0\\le u&lt;B\\), divisible by \\(B\\), greater than \\(2d\\), with\n\\[\n|H_u-H_v|&gt;2h+2\\max(H_{\\mathrm{ext}},w_0)+2\n\\quad(u\\ne v).\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nChoose a positive multiple \\(G\\) of \\(2B\\) such that\n\\[\nG&gt;10\\left(\\max_uH_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}+r+K+d+w_0+1\\right).\n\\tag{27}\n\\]</p><p>For an integer sampling origin \\(t\\), put\n\\[\no_j=(j+1)G,\\qquad z_j(t)=t+o_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nf_j^u(t)=\\text{the first inner marker in }[z_j(t),z_j(t)+H_u],\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\nreturning \\(\\bot\\) if none exists. Equal search values mean equal absolute positions, or both \\(\\bot\\).</p><p>The main cuts of block \\(j\\), \\(1\\le j\\le g\\), are\n\\[\nk=t+c_j+aB,\\qquad\nc_j=(j+\\tfrac12)G,\\qquad0\\le a&lt;R.\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nThe periodic cuts, for \\(0\\le j\\le g\\), \\(0\\le u&lt;B\\), are\n\\[\nk=z_j(t)+H_u+L_{\\mathrm{lag}}+aB,\n\\qquad0\\le a&lt;ndN_M.\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal H\\) be the finite set of all offsets \\(k-t\\) in these schedules, and choose\n\\[\nw&gt;\\max(2h,\\operatorname{diam}\\mathcal H)+1.\n\\tag{31}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All the choices above are simultaneously possible and depend only on \\(M,\\Sigma\\). Ordered distinct-slot differences in \\(\\mathcal P\\) are also nonzero and distinct modulo \\(B\\). Every outer offset in \\(\\mathcal H\\) is divisible by \\(B\\).</p><p>The schedules have the reading-side separation in Lemma 6.1. When an extended inner search is marker-free, they also provide the progression of cuts with fixed prefix product and full clock sweep in Lemma 6.2.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the residues in (16), if \\(i&gt;j\\), the integer difference is\n\\[\n(i-j)4N^2+(i-j)(i+j).\n\\]\nThe second summand lies strictly between zero and \\(2N^2\\). Division by \\(4N^2\\) determines \\(i-j\\); the remainder determines \\(i+j\\), hence \\(i,j\\). Positive differences are distinct, as are their negatives. All signed differences lie in an interval of length less than \\(B\\), and no positive difference equals a negative one. Their residues are therefore nonzero and distinct.</p><p>The choice of \\(\\mu\\) is not circular. The numbers \\(\\kappa,g,g'\\), and the spaces \\(V,U\\), precede \\(\\mu\\). The formula for \\(B\\) is polynomial in \\(\\mu\\), and \\(m\\) is a constant multiple of \\(B\\). The right side of (17) grows logarithmically while its left side grows linearly.</p><p>For the provisional clock, the Chinese remainder theorem and invertibility of \\(B\\) modulo each \\(n_i^0\\) show that\n\\[\n\\{\\varphi^0(aB):0\\le a&lt;R\\}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nis the full product grid with coordinate orders \\(n_i^0\\). The same is true of \\(R\\) consecutive representatives of any fixed residue class modulo \\(B\\). Choose the primes so large that this grid has covering radius less than \\(\\rho/3\\) and approximates each target strictly within the clock’s perturbation tolerance.</p><p>For each roster slot, the grid supplies an admissible integer in its reserved residue class. Adding multiples of \\(BR\\) preserves both its residue and provisional clock value. Insert successive positions far enough to the right that all new positive differences exceed all old positive differences by more than \\(2w_0+2\\). Differences between new differences are differences of earlier positions, which already have the required separation. Their negatives satisfy the same conditions. Leave integer gaps between successive blocks, allowing the boundaries \\(\\beta_j\\) to be chosen.</p><p>The early positions are now fixed before selecting \\(d,K,r\\). Approximate \\(1/n_i^0\\) by \\(a_i/n_i\\) using sufficiently large distinct primes \\(n_i\\) and rounding \\(n_i/n_i^0\\). Arbitrarily accurate approximations exist.</p><p>Make this approximation sufficiently close on the finite sets \\(\\mathcal P\\) and \\(\\{aB:0\\le a&lt;R\\}\\). Keeping every \\(\\varphi(p)\\) within the single tolerance of its target gives (23) by Proposition 4. Keeping the net values within the unused margin between its provisional covering radius and \\(\\rho\\) gives (24). Every translate of this net meets every clock ball of radius \\(\\rho\\).</p><p>Thus the final period has no small prime factors, while \\(R\\) and \\(w_0=BR\\) retain their earlier sizes. The remaining choices are successive lower bounds on integers, with stated divisibility conditions, so they can all be met.</p><p>Lemma 6.1 supplies reading-side separation and divisibility. Lemma 6.2 supplies the fixed-product clock sweep in the marker-free case. The finite monoid power identity (1), also used there, follows generally by finding two equal powers among \\(c^0,\\ldots,c^{|M|}\\); their exponent difference divides \\(N_M\\). The regular action is faithful by the displayed identity \\(e_{1_M}d=e_d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Inner cut schedules — original Lemma 5.1.** Use the parameters, searches, and schedules defined in Proposition 6.", "statement": "<p>**Inner cut schedules — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Use the parameters, searches, and schedules defined in Proposition 6.</p><p>At a main cut\n\\[\nk=t+c_j+aB,\\qquad0\\le a&lt;R,\n\\]\nall inner searches through index \\(j-1\\), including inspections, lie before \\(k\\). Searches from index \\(j\\) onward lie after \\(k\\), even at every hypothesized origin\n\\[\nk-c_j-a'B,\\qquad0\\le a'&lt;R,\n\\]\nand with any width index \\(u\\).</p><p>At every periodic cut of (30), the full search for \\(f_j^u(t)\\), with its inspections, lies before the cut. Every offset \\(k-t\\) in either schedule is divisible by \\(B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The rightmost inspection for a search through \\(j-1\\) is at most\n\\[\nt+jG+\\max_uH_u+r&lt;t+(j+\\tfrac12)G\n\\]\nby (27).</p><p>For a search from \\(j\\) onward at\n\\[\n\\tau=k-c_j-a'B,\n\\]\nthe leftmost possible inspection is at least\n\\[\n\\tau+(j+1)G-r\n=k+\\tfrac12G-a'B-r&gt;k,\n\\]\nbecause \\(a'B&lt;w_0\\) and \\(G/2&gt;w_0+r\\).</p><p>The periodic assertion follows from \\(L_{\\mathrm{lag}}&gt;r\\). Divisibility follows from \\(2B\\mid G\\) and the choices of \\(H_u,L_{\\mathrm{lag}}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Periodic cuts with fixed prefix product — original Lemma 5.2.** Fix \\(j,u,t\\) in the setting of Proposition 6.", "statement": "<p>**Periodic cuts with fixed prefix product — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><p>Fix \\(j,u,t\\) in the setting of Proposition 6. Put \\(z=z_j(t)\\), and suppose\n\\[\n[z,z+H_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}]\n\\]\ncontains no inner marker and all required inspections are available.</p><p>Then some \\(d'&lt;d\\) gives, with \\(k_0=z+H_u+L_{\\mathrm{lag}}\\), cuts\n\\[\nk=k_0+ad'N_MB,\\qquad0\\le a&lt;n,\n\\tag{32}\n\\]\nbelonging to schedule (30). For any fixed beginning \\(s\\le z\\), \\(T_{s,k}\\) is constant along this progression. Its clock values \\(\\varphi(k-t)\\) traverse a translate of the full image of \\(\\varphi\\), hence include a translate of the net in (24).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.2 gives one periodic continuation, of period \\(d'&lt;d\\), throughout\n\\[\n[z+d,z+H_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}-d+K).\n\\]\nThe \\(|M|\\) periods immediately before \\(k_0\\) lie there, since\n\\[\nk_0-|M|d'&gt;z+d\n\\]\nby (25). Every cut in (32) lies there as well, because\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{lag}}+(n-1)d'N_MB\n&lt;L_{\\mathrm{lag}}+ndN_MB\n&lt;H_{\\mathrm{ext}}-2d.\n\\]\nThese cuts belong to (30), since \\(ad'N_M&lt;ndN_M\\).</p><p>Let \\(c\\in M\\) be the product of one period in phase at \\(k_0\\). Among\n\\[\n1,c,\\ldots,c^{|M|}\n\\]\ntwo powers agree. Their exponent difference \\(v\\) satisfies \\(1\\le v\\le|M|\\), hence \\(v\\mid N_M\\). Multiplication by further powers gives\n\\[\nc^{i+N_M}=c^i\\qquad(i\\ge|M|).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nT_{s,k}\n=T_{s,k_0-|M|d'}c^{|M|+aN_MB}\n=T_{s,k_0-|M|d'}c^{|M|},\n\\]\nindependently of \\(a\\).</p><p>Every prime dividing \\(n\\) is greater than \\(d,N_M,B\\), so\n\\[\n\\gcd(d'N_MB,n)=1.\n\\]\nThe increments in (32) run through all residues modulo \\(n\\). Applying \\(\\varphi\\) proves the clock assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The margins are simultaneously available as specified in Lemma 7.1, and \\(E\\subseteq\\Sigma^+\\) is a prefix code.", "statement": "<p>**Episodes and a shared end — construction and results of original Section 6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix nonempty finite \\(\\Sigma\\), finite monoid \\(M\\), and morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the schedules, stencil \\(\\mathcal P\\), offsets \\(\\mathcal H\\), and parameters of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\Delta=(\\mathcal H-\\mathcal H)\\cup(\\mathcal P-\\mathcal P)\\cup\\{0\\}.\n  \\]</p><p>**End marker rule.**\nChoose\n\\[\nS_\\bullet&gt;4(h+w+1),\\qquad\nd_\\bullet&gt;10BS_\\bullet+10(h+w+1),\\qquad\nK_\\bullet&gt;3d_\\bullet^2,\n\\tag{33}\n\\]\nand use Proposition 5 independently to obtain end markers with radius \\(r_\\bullet\\ge K_\\bullet\\). Put\n\\[\nD_u^0=d_\\bullet+uS_\\bullet,\\qquad\nD_u^1=3d_\\bullet+uS_\\bullet\n\\quad(0\\le u&lt;B).\n\\tag{34}\n\\]\nFor \\(z_\\bullet(t)=t+o_\\bullet\\), define \\(\\eta(t)\\) and \\(\\zeta(t)\\), using \\(\\sigma=0,1\\), respectively, to return the leftmost end marker \\(m_e\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|m_e-z_\\bullet(t)|\\le D_u^\\sigma,\n\\qquad\nu=(m_e-z_\\bullet(t))\\bmod B.\n\\tag{35}\n\\]\nReturn \\(\\bot\\) if there is none.</p><p>If \\(\\eta(t)=\\bot\\), the seed\n\\[\n[z_\\bullet(t),z_\\bullet(t)+K_\\bullet)\n\\tag{37}\n\\]\nhas a unique two-sided continuation with some positive period below \\(d_\\bullet\\).</p><p>**Margins.**\nChoose a bounded offset interval \\([A_{\\mathrm{in}},B_{\\mathrm{in}}]\\), relative to \\(t\\), containing every inner window and cut needed at all displacements \\(\\delta\\in\\Delta\\) and all additional shifts in \\([-w_0,w_0]\\). Include all width indices, marker inspections, extended searches, short-period blocks, the finite continuation portions used in Lemma 6.2, and the \\(|M|\\) periods before its base cuts. In particular, include\n\\[\n[o_j+\\delta-v-r,\\,\n o_j+\\delta+v+H_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}+K+r]\n\\]\nfor \\(\\delta\\in\\Delta\\), \\(0\\le v\\le w_0\\).</p><p>Choose a bounded offset interval \\([A_{\\mathrm{end}},B_{\\mathrm{end}}]\\), relative to \\(z_\\bullet(t)\\), containing all end-search windows, seeds, possible bounded returns, and seed right endpoints for displacements \\(\\delta\\in\\Delta\\) and additional shifts \\(|v|\\le w\\). It suffices to include\n\\[\n[\\delta+v-\\max_uD_u^1-r_\\bullet,\\,\n \\delta+v+\\max(\\max_uD_u^1+r_\\bullet,K_\\bullet)].\n\\]\nThese intervals do not depend on \\(L,o_\\bullet,L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\). Choose these positive integers in that order so that\n\\[\nL+A_{\\mathrm{in}}&gt;\\beta_{g'},\\qquad\no_\\bullet+A_{\\mathrm{end}}&gt;B_{\\mathrm{in}},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{tail}}&gt;\n\\max\\{1,\\max_uD_u^0+B_{\\mathrm{end}}\\}.\n\\tag{38}\n\\]</p><p>**Episode rule.**\nFor a word beginning at \\(s\\), put \\(t=s+L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\eta(t)\\ne\\bot\\), set \\(q_0=\\eta(t)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Otherwise, let \\(q_0\\) be the first letter position at or beyond the seed’s right endpoint where the word differs from the seed’s periodic continuation.</p><p class=\"li\">If that mismatch has not occurred, there is no complete episode.</p><p class=\"li\">When \\(q_0\\) exists, the prescribed endpoint is\n  \\[\n  b=q_0+L_{\\mathrm{tail}}.\n  \\tag{39}\n  \\]\n  All required bounded inspections and seed data must be present, as must the letters through \\(b-1\\).</p><p>Let \\(E\\) be the words whose own endpoint is exactly this prescribed endpoint, with beginning normalized to zero. On each complete episode define the clock anchor\n\\[\nq=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\zeta(t),&amp;\\zeta(t)\\ne\\bot,\\\\\nq_0,&amp;\\zeta(t)=\\bot.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{40}\n\\]</p><p>**Common end guard.**\nFor a suffix beginning at an outer cut \\(k\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_k=\\{k-a:a\\in\\mathcal H\\}.\n\\tag{41}\n\\]\nChoose one fixed reference offset in \\(\\mathcal H\\). Require both \\(\\eta\\) and \\(\\zeta\\) to be constant over \\(\\mathcal O_k\\), with all bounded inspections and seeds present. Require the suffix to end exactly according to the episode rule at the reference origin, and use its clock anchor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The margins are simultaneously available as specified in Lemma 7.1, and \\(E\\subseteq\\Sigma^+\\) is a prefix code. At any genuine outer cut of the first episode of a word in \\(E^*\\), a successful common end guard forces its true endpoint and true clock anchor. The actual suffix satisfies the guard under the stability condition of Lemma 7.3.</p>", "proof": "<p>All end-search windows are bounded. The eligible set for \\(\\eta(t)\\) is contained in that for \\(\\zeta(t)\\), although the returned leftmost markers need not coincide. Moreover,\n\\[\n|t-t'|\\le2h,\\quad \\eta(t')\\ne\\bot\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\zeta(t)\\ne\\bot,\n\\tag{36}\n\\]\nbecause a marker returned at \\(t'\\) lies within\n\\[\n\\max_uD_u^0+2h\n&lt;3d_\\bullet\n=\\min_uD_u^1\n\\]\nof \\(z_\\bullet(t)\\), making it eligible regardless of its new residue index.</p><p>If \\(\\eta(t)=\\bot\\), no end marker lies in\n\\[\n[z_\\bullet(t)-d_\\bullet,z_\\bullet(t)+d_\\bullet].\n\\]\nLemma 5.1 gives the seed a period below \\(d_\\bullet\\), and Lemma 5.2 gives its unique short-period continuation.</p><p>The remaining choices of margins are lower bounds on successively chosen integers. Lemma 7.1 proves all required windows internal, Lemma 7.2 proves the prefix-code property, and Lemma 7.3 proves the common-end assertions.</p><p>The definitions commute with translation. A standalone suffix may specify an origin by a fixed offset from its own beginning even when the origin itself has no visible letter in the suffix. It must nevertheless contain all end searches and seed data and satisfy its own exact-end test; it is not asked to validate omitted prefix letters. The endpoint anchor \\(q_0\\) and clock anchor \\(q\\) remain distinct roles, and a present \\(\\zeta\\) need not equal \\(q_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Internal data under all prescribed hypotheses — original Lemma 6.1.** With the choices in Proposition 7, every bounded inner window lies after \\(b'_{g'}\\), every bounded end window lies after every outer cut, and all …", "statement": "<p>**Internal data under all prescribed hypotheses — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>With the choices in Proposition 7, every bounded inner window lies after \\(b'_{g'}\\), every bounded end window lies after every outer cut, and all these windows lie strictly inside every complete episode.</p><p>The assertions hold simultaneously for all displacements in \\(\\Delta\\) and additional shifts prescribed in Proposition 7.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first two inequalities in (38) prove the first two claims. In either branch of the episode rule,\n\\[\nq_0\\ge z_\\bullet(t)-\\max_uD_u^0.\n\\]\nThe last inequality in (38) therefore gives\n\\[\nb=q_0+L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\n&gt;z_\\bullet(t)+B_{\\mathrm{end}},\n\\]\nbeyond every bounded end window and hence every earlier window. The first inequality also places all these windows after \\(s=b'_0\\). The same bounds apply on any continuation of the word.</p><p>Every prefix or suffix test must have each required window and product interval in its own argument and on its own reading side. Missing inspections or invalid intervals cause rejection, not removal of an origin from a universal test. The unbounded part of the episode rule is a continuation comparison up to a proposed endpoint; it does not move an unbounded inspection window before a cut.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Prefix-code property — original Lemma 6.2.** The language \\(E\\) defined in Proposition 7 is a prefix code of nonempty words.", "statement": "<p>**Prefix-code property — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>The language \\(E\\) defined in Proposition 7 is a prefix code of nonempty words.</p>", "proof": "<p>Read a complete episode from its beginning in a longer word. Lemma 7.1 puts every bounded search and seed inspection inside the episode, so locality gives the same search values in the longer word.</p><p>If \\(\\eta\\) is present, it gives the same \\(q_0\\). If absent, the seed gives the same continuation, and the already encountered first mismatch remains the first mismatch. Thus \\(q_0\\), and hence \\(b\\), cannot change. No complete episode is a proper prefix of another.</p><p>Positivity follows from the strict ordering in Lemma 7.1 and \\(L_{\\mathrm{tail}}&gt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Shared end and clock anchor — original Lemma 6.3.** Let a word in \\(E^*\\) have first episode \\([s,b)\\), sampling origin \\(t=s+L\\), and clock anchor \\(q\\).", "statement": "<p>**Shared end and clock anchor — original Lemma 6.3.**</p><p>Let a word in \\(E^*\\) have first episode \\([s,b)\\), sampling origin \\(t=s+L\\), and clock anchor \\(q\\). Let \\(k=t+a\\) be a genuine outer cut, \\(a\\in\\mathcal H\\).</p><p>If a suffix starting at \\(k\\) satisfies the common end guard, its consumed endpoint is exactly \\(b\\), and its clock anchor is exactly \\(q\\).</p><p>Conversely, the actual suffix from \\(k\\) to \\(b\\) satisfies the guard whenever both end searches at \\(t\\) are unchanged under every shift divisible by \\(B\\) with absolute value at most \\(w\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Because \\(k=t+a\\) with \\(a\\in\\mathcal H\\), the set \\(\\mathcal O_k\\) contains \\(t\\). Its other origins differ from \\(t\\) by \\(\\mathcal H-\\mathcal H\\). Lemma 7.1 places all their bounded end data inside the first episode and after \\(k\\). A suffix must supply every window. If too short it rejects; otherwise it reads the genuine letters in those windows even when proposing a false endpoint.</p><p>If the common value of \\(\\eta\\) is a marker, it equals the actual \\(q_0\\), since \\(t\\) is among the origins. The reference exact-end test therefore demands \\(q_0+L_{\\mathrm{tail}}=b\\).</p><p>If every smaller search is absent, let \\(t_*\\) be the reference origin. Its seed and the true seed start less than \\(w&lt;d_\\bullet\\) apart, and overlap in at least\n\\[\nK_\\bullet-w&gt;d_\\bullet^2\n\\]\nletters. Lemma 5.2 identifies their periodic continuations. The word agrees with that continuation on both seeds and hence on their union. If one seed ends earlier, agreement continues through the end of the later seed. Their first subsequent mismatch is therefore the same position. An earlier alleged mismatch contradicts agreement or the true first-mismatch property; a later one passes the true mismatch. The reference test again forces \\(b\\).</p><p>The common value of \\(\\zeta\\) also forces \\(q\\): it is the actual bounded return when present, and the common \\(q_0\\) otherwise.</p><p>Conversely, each displacement from \\(t\\) to a member of \\(\\mathcal O_k\\) is divisible by \\(B\\) and has magnitude less than \\(w\\). The assumed stability makes both searches constant there. All windows are available by Lemma 7.1. A common marker return fixes the true end; in the absent case the seed argument fixes the common first mismatch. Thus the guard succeeds and supplies the true clock anchor.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every match of \\(P_xQ_y\\) beginning a word of \\(E^*\\) consumes exactly its first episode \\(e\\) and satisfies \\(F_e(x)=y\\).", "statement": "<p>**First split — original Proposition 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix nonempty finite \\(\\Sigma\\), finite monoid \\(M\\), and morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(V=\\mathbb F_2^M\\), the tags, clock, searches, and schedules of Proposition 6, and the episode code and anchors of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">For an episode \\(e=[s,b)\\), put \\(t=s+L\\).</p><p>Define \\(Z(t)\\) to mean that at least one of \\(\\eta,\\zeta\\) is not constant on\n\\[\n\\{t+v:v\\in B\\mathbb Z,\\ |v|\\le w\\}.\n\\]\nFor a position \\(Q\\), put \\(u=(Q-t)\\bmod B\\). Let \\(A_*(t,Q)\\) be the disjunction:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\varphi(Q-t)\\in W\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. for some \\(0\\le j\\le g\\) and \\(|v|\\le w_0\\),\n   \\[\n   f_j^u(t+v)\\ne f_j^u(t),\n   \\]\n   holding \\(u\\) fixed during the shift;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. for some \\(0\\le j\\le g\\), \\(f_j^u(t)=\\bot\\), but\n   \\[\n   [z_j(t),z_j(t)+H_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}]\n   \\]\n   contains an inner marker.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nD=\\{e\\in E:Z(t)\\text{ or }A_*(t,q)\\}.\n\\tag{42}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(A,C\\in M\\), define\n\\[\nf_{A,C}(a_1,\\ldots,a_g):\nv\\longmapsto vAa_1\\cdots a_gC,\n\\tag{43}\n\\]\nand use its fixed correction \\(\\beta_{f_{A,C}}\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p>Define a deterministic total affine update \\(F_e:V\\to V\\) on every \\(e\\in E\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(\\mathcal E_{\\varphi(q-t)}=\\{(\\alpha,\\lambda)\\}\\) is off-diagonal, put\n   \\[\n   F_e(v)=vT_{s,b}+Y(\\lambda)-X(\\alpha)T_{s,b}.\n   \\tag{44}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Otherwise put \\(u=(q-t)\\bmod B\\). If some \\(f_j^u(t)\\) is absent, put \\(F_e(v)=vT_{s,b}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Otherwise let \\(l_j=f_j^u(t)\\), \\(0\\le j\\le g\\), and put\n   \\[\n   A=T_{s,l_0},\\quad C=T_{l_g,b},\\quad\n   d_i=T_{l_{i-1},l_i}\\quad(1\\le i\\le g),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   F_e(v)=vf_{A,C}(d)+\\beta_{f_{A,C}}(d).\n   \\tag{45}\n   \\]</p><p>Define \\(P_x,Q_y\\subseteq\\Sigma^*\\), for \\(x,y\\in V\\), by finite unions over independently chosen tags:</p><p>**Common prefix tests.**\nA prefix on \\([s,k)\\) chooses a tag \\(\\alpha\\), requires\n\\[\nx=X(\\alpha),\\qquad \\varphi(k-t)\\in I_\\alpha,\n\\tag{46}\n\\]\nand chooses a cut of its tag’s form.</p><p>**Common suffix tests.**\nA suffix on \\([k,b)\\) independently chooses \\(\\lambda\\), performs the full common end guard over \\(\\{k-a:a\\in\\mathcal H\\}\\), and uses its resulting clock anchor \\(q\\). Require\n\\[\ny=Y(\\lambda),\\qquad\n\\varphi(q-k)\\in J_\\lambda,\\qquad\nu(\\lambda)=(q-k)\\bmod B.\n\\tag{47}\n\\]</p><p>**Main-tag tests.**\nFor a main tag \\((j,u,A,C,d_{-j},X,Y)\\):</p><p class=\"li\">The prefix chooses \\(k=t+c_j+aB\\), \\(0\\le a&lt;R\\), requires \\(f_0^u(t),\\ldots,f_{j-1}^u(t)\\) present, and checks \\(A\\) and the listed \\(d_i\\), \\(i&lt;j\\), on its side.</p><p class=\"li\">Writing \\(d[j\\leftarrow a]\\) for insertion of \\(a\\in M\\) into the omitted coordinate, the prefix requires\n  \\[\n  Xf_{A,C}(d[j\\leftarrow a])\n  +\\beta_{f_{A,C}}(d[j\\leftarrow a])=Y\n  \\quad(a\\in M).\n  \\tag{48}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The suffix requires searches \\(j,\\ldots,g\\), with its fixed \\(u\\), present and identical in absolute position at every origin\n  \\[\n  k-c_j-aB,\\qquad0\\le a&lt;R.\n  \\tag{49}\n  \\]\n  It checks the listed \\(d_i\\), \\(i&gt;j\\), and \\(C\\) using these common positions.</p><p>**Periodic-tag tests.**\nFor a periodic tag \\((j,u,m_p,X,Y)\\):</p><p class=\"li\">The prefix chooses\n  \\[\n  k=z_j(t)+H_u+L_{\\mathrm{lag}}+aB,\\qquad0\\le a&lt;ndN_M,\n  \\]\n  and checks \\(f_j^u(t)=\\bot\\) and \\(m_p=T_{s,k}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The suffix requires\n  \\[\n  Y=Xm_pT_{k,b}.\n  \\tag{50}\n  \\]</p><p>Every inspection and product interval must be available on the testing factor’s own reading side; otherwise that test rejects.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every match of \\(P_xQ_y\\) beginning a word of \\(E^*\\) consumes exactly its first episode \\(e\\) and satisfies \\(F_e(x)=y\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every \\(e\\in E\\setminus D\\) and every \\(x\\in V\\), a split witnessing\n   \\[\n   e\\in P_xQ_{F_e(x)}\n   \\]\n   exists.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. There is no match on the empty word.</p><p>Every \\(F_e\\), including those on \\(D\\), has linear part equal to the right action of \\(T_{s,b}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The episode determines \\(D\\) and \\(F_e\\) without choosing a split. The off-diagonal case is unambiguous by Proposition 4. The other two cases cover every graph contained in the diagonal, including the empty graph. In the third case,\n\\[\nAd_1\\cdots d_gC=T_{s,b}.\n\\]\nThus all cases are total affine maps with the prescribed linear part.</p><p>**Soundness.**\nLet independent tags \\(\\alpha,\\lambda\\) witness a successful pair. The prefix length is positive and, in a nonempty word of \\(E^*\\), ends at a scheduled cut strictly inside the first episode. Lemma 7.3 forces the true endpoint \\(b\\) and clock anchor \\(q\\), even if the tags propose different forms or indices. Positive prefix length excludes a match on \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>The clock tests now use common actual positions:\n\\[\n\\varphi(q-t)=\\varphi(k-t)+\\varphi(q-k)\\in I_\\alpha+J_\\lambda.\n\\]\nIf the clock graph is contained in the diagonal, \\(\\alpha=\\lambda\\). Since \\(k-t\\) is divisible by \\(B\\), the suffix residue field is the true \\(u=(q-t)\\bmod B\\).</p><p>For a main tag, the actual origin is among (49). The prefix verifies boundaries through \\(j-1\\), and the suffix those from \\(j\\) onward. Together they verify \\(A,C\\) and every listed interval product. All actual boundaries are present. Substitute the actual missing product in (48) to obtain \\(F_e(x)=y\\) under (45).</p><p>For an equal periodic tag, the prefix establishes absence of the actual search with true index \\(u\\), so the linear case applies. Its checked \\(m_p=T_{s,k}\\) and (50) give\n\\[\ny=xm_pT_{k,b}=xT_{s,b}=F_e(x).\n\\]\nAn empty graph permits no successful pair.</p><p>If the graph is a singleton off-diagonal edge, the pair is exactly that edge. The state tests give \\(x=X(\\alpha)\\), \\(y=Y(\\lambda)\\), and (44) gives \\(F_e(x)=y\\), without agreement of other fields.</p><p>**Completeness.**\nFix \\(e\\in E\\setminus D\\) and \\(x\\in V\\). The negation of \\(Z(t)\\) supplies the common end guard at every scheduled cut. Put \\(u=(q-t)\\bmod B\\). Since \\(\\varphi(q-t)\\notin W\\), every tag has a radius-\\(\\rho\\) ball in\n\\[\nI_\\alpha\\cap(\\varphi(q-t)-J_\\alpha),\n\\]\nand the graph is the full diagonal.</p><p>If every \\(f_i^u(t)\\) is present, take the actual products \\(A,C,d\\). Proposition 3 supplies an omitted coordinate \\(j\\) for this tuple and input. Choose the main tag with these true fields and \\(X=x\\), \\(Y=F_e(x)\\); its certificate holds. Its main-cut clock values are\n\\[\n\\varphi(c_j)+\\varphi(aB),\\qquad0\\le a&lt;R,\n\\]\na translate of the fixed net, so one meets its ball.</p><p>At that cut, every origin in (49) is\n\\[\nt+(a-a')B\n\\]\nwith displacement magnitude below \\(w_0\\). The stability part of \\(\\neg A_*(t,q)\\), holding \\(u\\) fixed, makes future marker positions agree. All remaining checks concern actual products on the correct sides, so the pair succeeds.</p><p>If some \\(f_j^u(t)\\) is absent, the last part of \\(\\neg A_*(t,q)\\) makes\n\\[\n[z_j(t),z_j(t)+H_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}]\n\\]\nmarker-free. Lemma 6.2 gives cuts\n\\[\nk_a=k_0+ad'N_MB,\\qquad0\\le a&lt;n,\\qquad\nk_0=z_j(t)+H_u+L_{\\mathrm{lag}},\n\\tag{51}\n\\]\nwhose prefix products share one value \\(m_p\\). Choose the single periodic tag with these \\(j,u,m_p\\), \\(X=x\\), and\n\\[\nY=xT_{s,b}=F_e(x).\n\\]\nConstancy of \\(m_p\\) uses eventual periodicity without cancellation. The clock values sweep a translate of the full image, hence include a translate of the net, so one meets the tag’s ball. The absence inspections lie before the cut, and\n\\[\nm_pT_{k_a,b}=T_{s,b}\n\\]\ngives its suffix certificate. The common end guard already holds.</p><p>Both cases give a split for every input \\(x\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If this suffix test is paired with a genuine stencil prefix \\([s,k)\\), \\(k=s+p\\), of the first episode \\(e\\in D\\) in a word of \\(D^*\\), success decodes the actual slot \\(p\\) and consumes exactly to the actual episode …", "statement": "<p>**Suffix decoder — original Lemma 8.3, using original Lemma 8.1 and Proposition 8.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix nonempty finite \\(\\Sigma\\), finite monoid \\(M\\), and morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the stencil \\(\\mathcal P\\), episode code \\(E\\), end searches, and margins of Propositions 6–7, and the domain \\(D\\) and predicates \\(Z,A_*\\) of Proposition 8.</p><p>For a standalone suffix beginning at \\(k\\), define one candidate origin for every slot:\n\\[\n\\tau_{p'}=k+L-p',\\qquad p'\\in\\mathcal P.\n\\tag{57}\n\\]\nAll inspections, including all shifted searches needed for \\(Z,A_*\\), are mandatory. Missing required windows reject the test.</p><p>The decoder performs these steps:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Compute every \\(\\zeta(\\tau_{p'})\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If some larger search is absent, require every \\(\\eta(\\tau_{p'})\\) absent and read every seed. At one fixed reference candidate require exact consumption according to its end rule: agreement with its periodic continuation up to the first subsequent mismatch, and exactly \\(L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\) letters from that mismatch to the proposed endpoint. Call its verified mismatch \\(q_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For each candidate set\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\tau_{p'}}=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\zeta(\\tau_{p'}),&amp;\\zeta(\\tau_{p'})\\ne\\bot,\\\\\n   q_{\\mathrm{ref}},&amp;\\zeta(\\tau_{p'})=\\bot.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   If all larger searches are present, every \\(Q_{\\tau_{p'}}\\) is its bounded return and no reference mismatch test is performed.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Require exactly one slot \\(p'\\), over the entire stencil without restriction on kinds, to satisfy\n   \\[\n   Z(\\tau_{p'})\\ \\text{or}\\ A_*(\\tau_{p'},Q_{\\tau_{p'}}).\n   \\tag{58}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. At the decoded origin require exact consumption according to its end rule, in both branches.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If this suffix test is paired with a genuine stencil prefix \\([s,k)\\), \\(k=s+p\\), of the first episode \\(e\\in D\\) in a word of \\(D^*\\), success decodes the actual slot \\(p\\) and consumes exactly to the actual episode endpoint \\(b\\).</p><p>Conversely, for any fixed \\(e\\in D\\), fewer than \\(\\mu\\) actual slots fail the decoder on their actual suffixes. In particular, every kind has a copy where decoding succeeds.</p>", "proof": "<p>**The true witness survives before uniqueness.**\nPair a proposed suffix with an actual cut \\(k=s+p\\). Its candidates are exactly\n\\[\nt+p-p',\\qquad p'\\in\\mathcal P,\n\\]\nincluding \\(t\\) at \\(p'=p\\). Every bounded inspection lies inside the actual first episode by Lemma 7.1 and is mandatory. Thus any suffix reaching uniqueness has read the actual bounded data regardless of its proposed end.</p><p>If some larger search is absent, the decoder requires all smaller searches absent and reads every seed, including the true seed. Their overlapping continuations coincide and agree on the union. The reference exact-end check therefore identifies the true first mismatch \\(q_0\\): an earlier mismatch contradicts agreement before \\(q_0\\), and a later one fails to detect \\(q_0\\). This branch already forces the true endpoint. All anchors agree with (53), so the true candidate satisfies the exceptional predicate.</p><p>If every larger search is present, each candidate anchor is a bounded return already read inside the actual episode. In particular,\n\\[\nQ_t=\\zeta(t)=q.\n\\]\nNo endpoint assumption is needed to evaluate the true predicate. Since \\(e\\in D\\), it again satisfies (58). This branch may still have an unbounded episode end when the true smaller search is absent.</p><p>**Uniqueness and endpoint soundness.**\nThe actual slot is a witness in either branch before uniqueness. Hence successful uniqueness decodes that slot. The final exact-end test is now at the true origin and forces \\(b\\). It is imposed in both branches, including the all-larger-searches-present branch.</p><p>**Completeness at almost every slot.**\nOn each actual suffix, Lemma 7.1 supplies all windows. If a larger search is absent, (52) makes all smaller searches absent, and the common-seed argument makes the reference test pass. The computed anchors are the restriction of (53) to that slot’s candidate list. Thus the true witness and final end check pass. Only another witness can prevent success.</p><p>For every unsuccessful actual slot \\(p\\), select another witnessing slot \\(p'\\ne p\\). The pair is counted in (54). Different unsuccessful slots have different first coordinates, so this selection is injective. Lemma 9.2 gives fewer than \\(\\mu\\) unsuccessful slots. Every kind has \\(\\mu\\) copies, leaving at least one successful copy of each.</p><p>The two end-search sizes ensure that the decoder never seeks an unverified future mismatch beyond a short true episode merely to evaluate a false origin: either one larger search is absent and the reference mismatch is the true one, or every candidate clock anchor is bounded data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Number of possible anchors — original Lemma 8.1.** Fix an actual complete episode \\([s,b)\\) with \\(t=s+L\\).", "statement": "<p>**Number of possible anchors — original Lemma 8.1.**</p><p>Fix an actual complete episode \\([s,b)\\) with \\(t=s+L\\). For all \\(p,p'\\in\\mathcal P\\), put\n\\[\n\\delta=p-p',\\qquad\\tau=t+\\delta.\n\\]\nEvaluate \\(\\zeta(\\tau)\\) on the actual episode.</p><p>If any such larger search is absent, all smaller searches \\(\\eta(\\tau)\\) in this collection are absent; their seeds have one common continuation and one common first subsequent mismatch \\(q_0\\), equal to the actual episode’s mismatch anchor.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nQ_\\tau=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\zeta(\\tau),&amp;\\zeta(\\tau)\\ne\\bot,\\\\\nq_0,&amp;\\zeta(\\tau)=\\bot.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{53}\n\\]\nThe second branch is needed only when a larger search is absent. In particular, \\(Q_t=q\\).</p><p>The values \\(Q_\\tau\\) belong to a set \\(\\mathcal Q\\) of at most \\(20\\) positions.</p>", "proof": "<p>All origins in the collection differ by at most \\(2h\\). If a marker is eligible for a smaller search at any one origin, it lies within\n\\[\n\\max_uD_u^0+2h&lt;\\min_uD_u^1\n\\tag{52}\n\\]\nof the end-search center at any other origin. It is therefore eligible for that larger search, irrespective of its new residue. An absent larger search consequently forces all smaller searches absent.</p><p>Their seeds start within diameter \\(2h\\) and overlap in at least \\(d_\\bullet^2\\) letters. Lemma 5.2 gives one continuation agreeing with the episode on their union. Their first subsequent mismatch is the same \\(q_0\\), which is the actual mismatch because the true smaller search is absent.</p><p>Every present \\(\\zeta(\\tau)\\) is an end marker in\n\\[\n[z_\\bullet(t)-h-\\max_uD_u^1,\\,\n z_\\bullet(t)+h+\\max_uD_u^1].\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\max_uD_u^1=3d_\\bullet+(B-1)S_\\bullet\n\\]\nand \\(d_\\bullet&gt;10BS_\\bullet+10(h+w+1)\\), this interval has length less than \\((31/5)d_\\bullet\\). Markers are separated by at least \\(d_\\bullet\\), so there are at most seven. There is at most one further value, the shared mismatch \\(q_0\\). The asserted bound of \\(20\\) follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Whole-stencil sparsity — original Proposition 8.2.** Fix a complete episode with sampling origin \\(t\\).", "statement": "<p>**Whole-stencil sparsity — original Proposition 8.2.**</p><p>Fix a complete episode with sampling origin \\(t\\). Define \\(Q_\\tau\\) for \\(\\tau=t+p-p'\\) as in Lemma 9.1: a present larger-search return is used, and otherwise the common actual mismatch is used.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\#\\left\\{(p,p')\\in\\mathcal P^2:\np\\ne p',\\\nZ(t+p-p')\\ \\text{or}\\\nA_*(t+p-p',Q_{t+p-p'})\n\\right\\}&lt;\\mu.\n\\tag{54}\n\\]\nThis counts all ordered distinct-slot pairs, independently of their metadata kinds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\delta=p-p'\\), \\(\\tau=t+\\delta\\). By the margins, all marker decisions, including shifted inspections, can be made in one common two-sided extension of the actual episode.</p><p>**End-search instability contributes at most four pairs.**\nIf an end search changes under a shift \\(v\\in B\\mathbb Z\\), \\(|v|\\le w\\), some marker’s eligibility changes. Its residue\n\\[\nu=(m_e-z_\\bullet(t)-\\delta)\\bmod B\n\\]\nis unchanged during the shift. Hence for some \\(\\sigma\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and one of two signs,\n\\[\n\\left|m_e-\\left(z_\\bullet(t)+\\delta\\pm D_u^\\sigma\\right)\\right|\\le w.\n\\tag{55}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\sigma\\) and sign, all possible markers in (55) lie in an interval of diameter at most\n\\[\n2h+(B-1)S_\\bullet+2w&lt;d_\\bullet.\n\\]\nThere is at most one end marker there. For that marker, different distinct-slot differences give distinct indices \\(u\\), since those differences are distinct modulo \\(B\\). Two targets in (55) are separated by at least\n\\[\nS_\\bullet-2h&gt;2w.\n\\]\nAt most one pair satisfies (55) for each search and sign. The total is at most four.</p><p>**Clock failures for a fixed anchor.**\nFor each \\(Q\\in\\mathcal Q\\), the clock part of \\(A_*(\\tau,Q)\\) is\n\\[\n\\varphi(Q-t)+\\varphi(p')-\\varphi(p)\\in W.\n\\]\nBy (23), it counts at most \\(c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\log(2m)\\) ordered distinct-slot pairs. Uniformity in the translate allows every actual \\(Q\\), including an unbounded mismatch. Lemma 9.1 permits at most \\(20\\) applications.</p><p>**Inner-marker failures for a fixed anchor and label.**\nFix \\(Q\\in\\mathcal Q\\), \\(j\\in\\{0,\\ldots,g\\}\\), and put\n\\[\nz=z_j(t)+\\delta,\\qquad\nu=(Q-t-\\delta)\\bmod B.\n\\]\nDuring the stability test, hold \\(u\\) fixed and shift \\(z\\) by at most \\(w_0\\).</p><p>The union of all possible left-endpoint positions has diameter\n\\[\n2h+2w_0&lt;d,\n\\]\nso contains at most one inner marker. The distinct differences \\(\\delta\\) are separated by more than \\(2w_0+2\\). At most one pair has its left endpoint within \\(w_0\\) of that marker; charge it to the left endpoint.</p><p>For every remaining pair, no marker crosses the left endpoint during allowed shifts. Thus the first marker on or after \\(z\\), if any, is fixed. A search change can occur only if it lies within \\(w_0\\) of \\(z+H_u\\). If the original search is absent but the extended search contains a marker, its first marker lies in\n\\[\n(z+H_u,z+H_u+H_{\\mathrm{ext}}].\n\\]\nEither failure places the first marker on or after \\(z\\) within \\(\\max(H_{\\mathrm{ext}},w_0)\\) of \\(z+H_u\\).</p><p>As \\(z\\) ranges over a sweep of diameter \\(2h&lt;d\\), at most two markers can play this role: a marker within the sweep, and the first marker after it. Different distinct-slot differences give distinct \\(u\\). By (26),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;|(z_j(t)+\\delta+H_u)-(z_j(t)+\\delta'+H_{u'})|\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\ge |H_u-H_{u'}|-|\\delta-\\delta'|\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad&gt;2\\max(H_{\\mathrm{ext}},w_0)+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEach possible first marker accounts for at most one pair. Including the possible left-endpoint pair gives at most three pairs for fixed \\(Q,j\\).</p><p>Combining all counts bounds the left side of (54) by\n\\[\n4+20\\bigl(c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\log(2m)+3(g+1)\\bigr)\n&lt;\n20\\bigl(c_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\log(2m)+4(g+1)\\bigr)+10\n&lt;\\mu.\n\\tag{56}\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(Q\\), counting all pairs rather than only those assigned that \\(Q\\) is an upper bound. Taking the union over \\(\\mathcal Q\\) proves the claim over the whole stencil.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Each \\(G_e\\) is a deterministic total affine map on \\(U\\) with linear part \\(\\widehat F_e\\).", "statement": "<p>**Second split — original Proposition 9.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix nonempty finite \\(\\Sigma\\), finite monoid \\(M\\), and morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the stencil and boundaries of Proposition 6, the episode code and margins of Proposition 7, the exceptional domain \\(D\\) and affine updates \\(F_e\\) of Proposition 8, and the decoder of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V=\\mathbb F_2^M\\), \\(U=V\\oplus\\mathbb F_2\\). Write \\(\\widehat F_e\\) for the homogeneous lift.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(e=[s,b)\\in D\\), put \\(b'_* = b'_{g'}\\).</p><p>**A total table from later letters.**\nFor every \\(m'\\in M\\), let\n\\[\nD_b=T_{b'_*,b},\\qquad m_b(m')=m'D_b.\n\\]\nDefine \\(H_{e,m'}:V\\to V\\) by the same three cases as \\(F_e\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If the actual clock graph is the singleton off-diagonal edge \\(\\{(\\alpha,\\lambda)\\}\\), set\n   \\[\n   H_{e,m'}(v)=vm_b(m')+Y(\\lambda)-X(\\alpha)m_b(m').\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Otherwise use \\(u=(q-t)\\bmod B\\). If some actual \\(f_j^u(t)\\) is absent, set\n   \\[\n   H_{e,m'}(v)=vm_b(m').\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Otherwise write the actual boundaries \\(l_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;l_g\\), and set\n   \\[\n   A(m')=m'T_{b'_*,l_0},\\qquad\n   C=T_{l_g,b},\\qquad\n   d_i=T_{l_{i-1},l_i},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   H_{e,m'}(v)\n   =vf_{A(m'),C}(d)+\\beta_{f_{A(m'),C}}(d).\n   \\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\psi_e:M\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{lin}}(U),\n\\qquad\n\\psi_e(m')=\\widehat H_{e,m'}.\n\\tag{60}\n\\]\nFor any proposed suffix origin, use the same formulas once its end rule and required data are available. Missing or wrongly ordered required intervals reject the whole suffix test; no origin or individual \\(m'\\) is discarded.</p><p>**Inner update.**\nPut\n\\[\nd'_i=T_{b'_{i-1},b'_i}\\qquad(1\\le i\\le g').\n\\]\nFor any total table \\(\\psi:M\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{lin}}(U)\\), define\n\\[\nf'_\\psi(a_1,\\ldots,a_{g'})=\\psi(a_1\\cdots a_{g'}).\n\\tag{62}\n\\]\nUsing the fixed correction from Proposition 3, define\n\\[\nG_e(x)=xf'_{\\psi_e}(d')+\\beta_{f'_{\\psi_e}}(d'),\n\\qquad x\\in U.\n\\tag{63}\n\\]</p><p>**Inner split languages.**\nFor \\(x,y\\in U\\):</p><p class=\"li\">A prefix in \\(\\widetilde P_x\\) chooses a slot \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\) and has exactly length \\(p\\). If its kind is \\((j,x_p,y_p,a_{-j},\\psi)\\), require \\(x_p=x\\), check the listed earlier products \\(a_i=d'_i\\), \\(i&lt;j\\), and require\n  \\[\n  x_pf'_\\psi(a[j\\leftarrow a])\n  +\\beta_{f'_\\psi}(a[j\\leftarrow a])\n  =y_p\n  \\quad(a\\in M).\n  \\tag{64}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A suffix in \\(\\widetilde Q_y\\) performs the full decoder, including every required reference end test and its final decoded-origin end test. For decoded slot \\(p\\) of kind \\((j,x_p,y_p,a_{-j},\\psi)\\), compute every entry of (60) at that origin and require equality with \\(\\psi\\) on all \\(m'\\in M\\). Check \\(a_i=d'_i\\), \\(i&gt;j\\), and require \\(y_p=y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All required intervals must be available in the factor’s own argument.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(G_e\\) is a deterministic total affine map on \\(U\\) with linear part \\(\\widehat F_e\\). The languages \\(\\widetilde P_x,\\widetilde Q_y\\) satisfy:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every match of \\(\\widetilde P_x\\widetilde Q_y\\) beginning a word in \\(D^*\\) consumes exactly its first episode \\(e\\) and has \\(G_e(x)=y\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every \\(e\\in D\\) admits such a split for every \\(x\\in U\\).</p><p>Thus this split has empty skip domain, and there is no successful pair on the empty word.</p>", "proof": "<p>If\n\\[\nF_e(v)=vL_e+\\gamma_e,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\widehat F_e(v,c)=(vL_e+c\\gamma_e,c).\n\\tag{59}\n\\]\nAll marker searches, outer boundaries, and end data lie after \\(b'_*\\) by Lemma 7.1. Hence the suffix beginning at \\(b'_*\\), together with its origin and anchors, determines the table (60).</p><p>The table is defined for every \\(m'\\), including impossible prefix products. In the third case, associativity gives\n\\[\nA(m')d_1\\cdots d_gC\n=m'T_{b'_*,l_0}T_{l_0,l_g}T_{l_g,b}\n=m'T_{b'_*,b}\n=m_b(m').\n\\]\nThus every affine entry and lift is defined. Substituting the actual early product only after defining the full table gives\n\\[\n\\psi_e(T_{s,b'_*})=\\widehat F_e.\n\\tag{61}\n\\]\nEvery letter-dependent quantity is read after \\(b'_*\\); \\(m'\\) is a finite algebraic input, not a request to find an omitted prefix. The table computation makes no call to a graph language for episode sequences.</p><p>Since\n\\[\nd'_1\\cdots d'_{g'}=T_{s,b'_*},\n\\]\nequation (61) shows that the linear part of \\(G_e\\) is \\(\\widehat F_e\\). Its correction is fixed, so \\(G_e\\) is total and deterministic.</p><p>A slot of block \\(j\\) lies inside \\((b'_{j-1},b'_j)\\). Every listed block product except \\(j\\) is therefore readable on one side of the cut. In particular, \\(b'_*\\) is after every slot, at a fixed positive offset from its suffix beginning, and all table data occur after that boundary.</p><p>**Soundness.**\nAn arbitrary successful prefix chooses a genuine stencil length and ends inside the first episode. Proposition 9 forces its paired suffix to decode that very slot and end at the actual episode endpoint. Both factors consequently use one kind, although that was not assumed when defining them.</p><p>The prefix verifies listed earlier products; the suffix verifies listed later products and the entire actual table \\(\\psi_e\\). The omitted actual product \\(d'_j\\) belongs to \\(M\\), so substituting it in the universal certificate (64) yields \\(G_e(x)=y\\). Positive stencil lengths exclude \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>**Completeness.**\nFix \\(e\\in D\\) and \\(x\\in U\\). Apply Proposition 3 to the total function \\(f'_{\\psi_e}\\) at the actual tuple \\(d'\\). Some coordinate \\(j\\) makes the corrected output constant, with value \\(G_e(x)\\).</p><p>Choose the kind with that coordinate, the actual other products, the entire actual table \\(\\psi_e\\), and fields \\(x,G_e(x)\\). It is in the roster because every total table was included. Its certificate holds for every missing value. Proposition 9 leaves at least one usable copy of this kind. At that copy, all readable product checks, the table comparison, and the end tests pass.</p><p>This works for every input; neither the omitted coordinate nor the copy is required to be common to different inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The episode language \\(E\\), its skipped sublanguage \\(D\\), and every suffix language in either split have generalized star height at most one over \\(\\Sigma\\).", "statement": "<p>**Height bounds for individual components — original Proposition 10.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a nonempty finite alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\), a finite monoid \\(M\\), and a morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fully fixed schedules, episode rule, domain \\(D\\), and outer and inner split languages constructed in Propositions 6–10.</p><p class=\"li\">Every component is regarded as a standalone language over \\(\\Sigma\\), with its own reading-side requirements and exact-end tests.</p><p class=\"li\">All complements are relative to \\(\\Sigma^*\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The episode language \\(E\\), its skipped sublanguage \\(D\\), and every suffix language in either split have generalized star height at most one over \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p>Every prefix language in either split has height zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every prefix length belongs to a fixed finite schedule. The corresponding prefix language is finite, hence has height zero.</p><p>For the other components, first obtain a finite template cover from their own exact-end checks. If a smaller end search returns a marker, its offset and the resulting endpoint are bounded. If absent, its fixed-position seed has a short-period continuation. Make finite choices of the word \\(a\\) from the argument’s own beginning through the seed’s right endpoint, the period and phase, and the tail of length \\(L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\) beginning at the first mismatch. Write the interval before that mismatch as full periods plus a partial period, placing the fixed partial period and tail in \\(b\\). Require the tail’s first letter to differ from the continuation. This gives finitely many templates \\(ac^ib\\), with bounded cases handled separately.</p><p>The languages \\(E,D\\) use the episode rule. An outer suffix uses the reference-origin exact-end check of its common guard. An inner suffix always checks its decoded origin’s exact end and sometimes also the reference origin’s end. Separate the finitely many possible decoded origins into cases. This template cover applies to each standalone suffix, including false origins or inconsistent tag fields; no ambient-episode assumption is used.</p><p>Multiple end guards do not introduce another unbounded search. Every mismatch-based guard requires its mismatch at the proposed argument end minus \\(L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\). After finite seed and period choices, it tests agreement up to that position and disagreement there. Both reference and decoded-origin guards are therefore comparisons allowed in Lemma 11.1. Missing windows reject rather than making the argument conditional on unread letters.</p><p>It remains to verify the calculation on each cover:</p><p class=\"li\">Every schedule, hypothesis, translated origin, stability shift, and inspection radius belongs to fixed finite data. Even a bounded integer shift range is a fixed finite list.</p><p class=\"li\">A bounded marker search selects one of finitely many offsets or returns absence. First-marker choices, equality comparisons, and availability requirements are finite calculations on bounded windows.</p><p class=\"li\">A clock anchor \\(Q_\\tau\\) is either a bounded larger-search return or the mismatch supplied by an exact-end test. In the latter case it is the argument end minus \\(L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\). Thus residues modulo \\(B\\), values under periodic \\(\\varphi\\), and the predicates \\(Z,A_*\\) use only the listed data.</p><p class=\"li\">Universal and uniqueness tests in the decoder are finite. Reference and decoded-origin continuation comparisons are allowed by Lemma 11.1 and never require a search past the proposed endpoint.</p><p class=\"li\">Outer product intervals have endpoints at boundedly selected searches, cuts, or the prescribed end. Inner stencil boundaries have fixed offsets under each origin hypothesis.</p><p class=\"li\">Computing every \\(\\psi_e(m')\\), \\(m'\\in M\\), uses products from the final stencil boundary to later selected boundaries or the end, together with bounded marker and clock data. The hypothetical \\(m'\\) is accepted as an algebraic input without asking whether a prefix realizing it exists.</p><p class=\"li\">All correction-table certificates range over fixed finite monoids and spaces. No component calls a graph-language test for a sequence of episodes.</p><p>Lemma 11.1 therefore gives height-one expressions for \\(E,D\\) and all suffix languages. Tags, states, and residues index only finite operations. Every word inside a template, including \\(c^\\ell\\) under its star, is over \\(\\Sigma\\); no height-preserving alphabet substitution is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The accepted words have a generalized expression of height at most one over \\(\\Sigma\\).", "statement": "<p>**Compilation on periodic templates — original Lemma 10.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\), a finite monoid \\(M\\), and a morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider finitely many templates\n  \\[\n  ac^ib,\\qquad i\\ge0,\n  \\tag{65}\n  \\]\n  where \\(a,b,c\\in\\Sigma^*\\) are fixed for each template and \\(c\\ne\\varepsilon\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On each template, acceptance is determined by a fixed finite calculation using:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. letters in finitely many bounded windows at fixed integer offsets from the argument’s beginning or end;</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. order and availability of positions chosen from fixed finite sets of such offsets, with missing required data causing rejection;</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. monoid products on available ordered intervals between those positions, and lengths or differences modulo fixed positive integers;</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. agreement of an interval between those positions with one of finitely many fixed periodic continuations, including any required mismatch letter at its endpoint.</p><p class=\"li\">Finite choices, universal tests, and uniqueness tests on fixed finite lists are allowed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The accepted words have a generalized expression of height at most one over \\(\\Sigma\\). Adding finitely many exceptional words preserves this conclusion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix one template \\(ac^ib\\). For sufficiently large \\(i\\), the relative order and availability of all bounded-offset positions are fixed. Adding another whole copy of \\(c\\) leaves bounded windows near either end unchanged. Products between endpoints near the same end are constant. Any product crossing the growing middle has the form\n\\[\nu\\,T(c)^{i-i_0}v\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(u,v\\in M\\) and integer \\(i_0\\), once \\(i\\) is large enough. Finite-monoid powers are ultimately periodic, so all these products and fixed-modulus length data are ultimately periodic in \\(i\\).</p><p>For a comparison with a continuation of period \\(d_0\\), separate the finitely many classes of \\(i\\bmod d_0\\). On a class, every bounded endpoint has fixed phase. Bounded-piece comparisons stabilize.</p><p>A growing middle compares repetition of \\(c\\) with the continuation in a fixed phase. If they disagree, some disagreement occurs within \\(|c|d_0\\) consecutive positions, a common period of the two two-sided repetitions. Every sufficiently long middle then fails. If no disagreement occurs in such a block, the whole growing comparison agrees, leaving only fixed beginning and end comparisons. A prescribed first mismatch is agreement up to the specified endpoint followed by a differing letter, so it is covered too. Bounded comparison intervals reduce to the window case.</p><p>There are finitely many data and phase cases. Beyond a common threshold their entire array has a common period. Any fixed calculation on the array, including universal or exactly-one tests, therefore has an ultimately periodic set of accepted exponents.</p><p>Such a set is a finite set together with finitely many progressions\n\\[\ni_1+\\ell N,\\qquad N\\ge0,\\quad\\ell&gt;0.\n\\]\nA progression is expressed by\n\\[\nac^{i_1}(c^\\ell)^*b.\n\\]\nIts fixed powers are words over \\(\\Sigma\\), so the height is one. Finite exceptional words and finite unions over progressions and templates preserve the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The language \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\) has height at most one.", "statement": "<p>**Incomplete remainder and unique factorization — original Lemma 10.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix nonempty finite \\(\\Sigma\\), finite monoid \\(M\\), and morphism \\(T:\\Sigma^*\\to M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be the episode prefix code of Proposition 7, with the expression bound from Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  N_{\\mathrm{end}}=\\neg(E\\,\\neg0).\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\) is the language of words with no complete episode prefix, since \\(\\neg0=\\Sigma^*\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The language \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\) has height at most one. For every \\(m_0\\in M\\),\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{end}}\\cap T^{-1}(m_0)\n\\]\nalso has height at most one.</p><p>Every word has a unique factorization into complete episodes followed by a word in \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(\\neg0\\) has height zero and \\(E\\) has height at most one, the displayed expression for \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\) has height at most one.</p><p>Choose a fixed length threshold beyond every bounded decision window for an episode beginning at zero and every possible marker-present episode endpoint. A longer word in \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\) cannot have a present smaller end search, because its prescribed complete episode would already be a prefix.</p><p>Its available seed therefore has a short-period continuation. Either there is no mismatch before the word ends, or its first mismatch \\(q_0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|w|-q_0&lt;L_{\\mathrm{tail}}.\n\\]\nOtherwise the prefix ending at \\(q_0+L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\) would be a complete episode. The margins ensure that all required bounded data are internal even when the mismatch is as early as allowed.</p><p>In the no-mismatch case, finite choices of beginning through the seed, period, phase, and final partial period give templates (65). In the mismatch case, append a portion of length less than \\(L_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\) beginning at the mismatch, chosen from a finite set. These are also such templates. Shorter words form a finite language.</p><p>On every template, the monoid-value test is ultimately periodic by Lemma 11.1. Form the height-one expression for value \\(m_0\\) on this template cover, then intersect it with \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\). This gives exactly\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{end}}\\cap T^{-1}(m_0)\n\\]\nat height at most one.</p><p>Repeatedly remove an episode prefix whenever one exists. Episodes are nonempty, so the process terminates in \\(N_{\\mathrm{end}}\\). The prefix-code property makes each removed episode unique. An alternative factorization cannot stop earlier, since its alleged remainder would still have an episode prefix. This proves unique factorization.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [5, 18, "cite"], [5, 22, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 14, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [10, 17, "cite"], [10, 20, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [11, 18, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [11, 22, "cite"], [11, 23, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 19, "cite"], [14, 22, "cite"], [14, 23, "cite"], [14, 25, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 14, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 22, "cite"], [18, 23, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [19, 23, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"]]});
